{:db/id 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124278 :question/id #uuid "f44b6ea6-694c-5f54-94ee-4c4a699bf4eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3696" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t  \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] .$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t, y=\\cos t,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186124960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde96964-49e5-5f8c-9a85-bf83f3f624a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id 17592186122361} {:answer/id #uuid "c18873f1-6416-5e3f-84a8-30f0d1e34a57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186122362} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8b5412-a282-5231-a52a-c430c381a304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id 17592186122363} {:answer/id #uuid "55bd4f06-001b-5b40-9449-067e29d3fd21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186122364} {:answer/id #uuid "5c13ba27-5c90-535c-9ea9-9232444e86bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186122365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde96964-49e5-5f8c-9a85-bf83f3f624a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id 17592186122361} :answer-field/id #uuid "30ce640c-974a-53b3-8a92-6e2b4d383ffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186122360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e234e1ce-e3a5-5460-9b85-a8876cb6e36c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36247" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+3xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t+1,-t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+3xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x+3yz,3xz,3xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=t+1,$ and $z=-t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2({t}^{2})+3(t+1)(-t),3({t}^{2})(-t),3({t}^{2})(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2}-3{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =(-{t}^{2}-3t)\\cdot 2t+(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot 1+(3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}-3{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-8{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =-8{(1)}^{3}-9{(1)}^{2}=-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186124961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b632d6e-7a98-53fb-a62b-8c9001dbea44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186122369} {:answer/id #uuid "019ba0e0-ece3-5798-adda-30d5f0d23288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2" :db/id 17592186479360} {:answer/id #uuid "3c938f80-1a2e-5bdc-937f-bbe6aa683da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡8" :db/id 17592186479449} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8f5ab1-0a45-584c-b611-498bfb7e8229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2" :db/id 17592186479497} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd3d589-8795-5da6-b2f2-1c064d2c6d7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2" :db/id 17592186479672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f8f5ab1-0a45-584c-b611-498bfb7e8229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2" :db/id 17592186479497} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbe9c851-9ada-5f1b-ae5b-56517e51d11b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186122366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8571bcaa-6cdf-5385-98c4-d1ed2624cfd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-36253" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},4t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=4t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},4⟩ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{1}-4\\cdot 1)(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}-4) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186284587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "945b9c04-6a85-5d2f-aac7-20fb300b68cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186282964} {:answer/id #uuid "a0759ec4-44c2-5df4-8049-d2141efec4e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id 17592186283086} {:answer/id #uuid "cefe4dd8-87f4-5862-998f-f2f8be85aafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id 17592186283496} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5f7e03-4f52-5b2c-83bb-c9656091962b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186283757} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bb78c7-3837-59e6-b577-9cf297b1d8d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186283790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec5f7e03-4f52-5b2c-83bb-c9656091962b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186283757} :answer-field/id #uuid "d53902b6-01db-5851-b51a-31934c273c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b00d973d-5785-5a66-98c1-0a412869e903" :question/math-academy-id "q-167499" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ at the point $t=9.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y},-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=\\sqrt{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(9))] & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186323770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a620de-8bd0-57cc-9ca8-627d570b10b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-2}{2}" :db/id 17592186321285} {:answer/id #uuid "539a1518-741f-5932-ab88-ffb0b81d4bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id 17592186321805} {:answer/id #uuid "6d69e5bf-08b0-519c-bd8e-5f0d93d50b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id 17592186321997} {:answer/id #uuid "ac78bbbd-2aa6-5d72-9963-b167b6edf486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}" :db/id 17592186322497} {:answer/id #uuid "c761e53e-5917-50ee-b4b8-03842b140243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186322693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac78bbbd-2aa6-5d72-9963-b167b6edf486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}" :db/id 17592186322497} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a86fdda-9d41-5703-bec7-0aa41e4dbc4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c3c463e-b1e6-5da9-a9e8-9ab02a63ca2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36227" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{x}^{2},2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186360526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65cdcc58-be4b-5338-b00a-ddcb5371bd93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186358643} {:answer/id #uuid "6b40bd8f-7feb-594d-94b7-c6fe911edb9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186358691} {:answer/id #uuid "743c9228-cbd8-5669-88ae-262b1ab32094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186358757} {:answer/id #uuid "93cbeb11-d6d7-50dd-9acd-c56791d17285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186359047} {:answer/id #uuid "bd23f796-bcb5-5c88-998f-bd753a03f298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186359399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b40bd8f-7feb-594d-94b7-c6fe911edb9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186358691} :answer-field/id #uuid "e85aac1a-a384-579c-bfda-1155a57b6d9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8defdc8d-5773-5ad6-96bf-f8efe589fed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94884" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t-1⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=2t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2{t}^{2},-2(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,2⟩ \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t+(2-4t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-8t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =4(0)^{3}-8(0)+4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186360527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ecb641-5c9e-59d2-9af0-e8b4b2b12cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id 17592186358237} {:answer/id #uuid "3913aa2e-745b-50de-97ac-427a88ec41d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id 17592186358256} {:answer/id #uuid "58cc3bc9-ecef-54de-9839-9c06a26426b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186358521} {:answer/id #uuid "996b9c09-c035-53fe-ba0a-1216f4518389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id 17592186359098} {:answer/id #uuid "dd14b83a-9e9f-5129-b7b0-19250eeeb3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id 17592186359698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996b9c09-c035-53fe-ba0a-1216f4518389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id 17592186359098} :answer-field/id #uuid "31bbcbbe-389e-5798-9aef-8032762b891a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53f0d6f9-0bd0-5fbc-870f-a5ea8fa6cdba" :question/math-academy-id "q-94887" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t},3t-1⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t,y=\\sqrt{t}$ and $z=3t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\sqrt{t}(3t-1),2t(3t-1),2t\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩ \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 2+(6{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+2t\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}+6t\\sqrt{t} \\\\  & =12t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =12(1)\\sqrt{1}-2\\sqrt{1}+\\frac{3(1)^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186360528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e78089-dc3f-5261-a0b9-838d20687d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id 17592186357803} {:answer/id #uuid "50e71c08-98f9-50a2-a32b-d087d082f280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186358447} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9f48cc-cae9-5cde-af64-d389fb6174af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186358832} {:answer/id #uuid "b8de77d8-406c-5b9b-a817-38720fcff979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186359354} {:answer/id #uuid "eec515f8-b1e7-52bb-b00a-3fe712040562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id 17592186359881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8de77d8-406c-5b9b-a817-38720fcff979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186359354} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f2da6b-5bbf-5958-acfc-6db810c9ab3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "102aa53b-1989-56be-b06a-9a6f6770770c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94888" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,4,\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},3{y}^{2},2xz⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=4$ and $z=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3})^{2},3(4)^{2},2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-3)\\cdot 1+48\\cdot 0+2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-3+0+2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =3(2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =12-3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186360529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b55c69-c88a-5b54-9cd7-12d2acc90eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id 17592186358067} {:answer/id #uuid "46655cc2-46c6-57d9-aae7-530ec487a9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id 17592186358354} {:answer/id #uuid "5de3fd27-1a74-5e78-9c11-0753f0b2af77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{17}" :db/id 17592186358573} {:answer/id #uuid "97ab5991-5eae-5b87-9b48-34164ebe108a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id 17592186359079} {:answer/id #uuid "a69903fd-123e-53f6-9cf8-6199c101e2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186359192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97ab5991-5eae-5b87-9b48-34164ebe108a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id 17592186359079} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8f9bfb-8eb2-5607-8ba8-b9429f52c201" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "530f5767-4f31-5ef7-a6dd-bdeac420a724" :question/math-academy-id "q-94891" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-1,3,2t⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t-1,$ $y=3$ and $z=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(3)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(2t)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 1+\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 0+\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2+8t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =\\frac{10(0)-2}{5(0)^{2}-2(0)+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186445680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc8881d-ce79-5fa4-94b3-93194df95932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id 17592186445652} {:answer/id #uuid "76ff76d1-a44d-5785-aeb6-f49cd08b8d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id 17592186445657} {:answer/id #uuid "869d2a2c-eca1-5a81-9c8c-bd38ea652d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id 17592186445658} {:answer/id #uuid "9980bb90-a6ef-51f4-88b3-4812a2e11e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(2\\pi )" :db/id 17592186445662} {:answer/id #uuid "e898d08a-c5cb-5c48-be98-c6dd2a755c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{ln}^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id 17592186445670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bc8881d-ce79-5fa4-94b3-93194df95932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id 17592186445652} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ea0794-709e-5625-aee2-f32cf13014aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186445676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b98d6bad-fbc9-5978-8040-a1a35d995f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-36234" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,2t⟩$ at the point $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{t}+2{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{\\pi }+2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186479863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bcd1713-5761-536d-9be9-d421928b31e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id 17592186479377} {:answer/id #uuid "49414ddc-847d-5122-866b-0471fb4d5ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{26}" :db/id 17592186479467} {:answer/id #uuid "84115f86-a2a8-5473-a1e9-46a7c9992127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" :db/id 17592186479553} {:answer/id #uuid "85155c3e-a79b-505c-a718-14f3a3535182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}" :db/id 17592186479556} {:answer/id #uuid "c9baafd5-f541-5292-ba8c-6d10037ebe04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id 17592186479666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49414ddc-847d-5122-866b-0471fb4d5ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{26}" :db/id 17592186479467} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6277da0-e2f4-5895-b1b1-64bd47ad216a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186479795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68446125-1b3e-53ed-aad0-d4e0129128a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94885" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,3t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=3t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{5}{1+25(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186479876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed77687-b07d-532f-8400-2a71d4a5ad46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186479380} {:answer/id #uuid "74be835c-e26b-5172-ac8b-75460bf573b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186479537} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9ac26e-5d8d-5f86-b225-32abc20a36ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186479573} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a561f8-22fa-5008-be18-18d377fa405a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186479658} {:answer/id #uuid "caa5e437-184f-52d9-8cfa-224935e09da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186479667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e9ac26e-5d8d-5f86-b225-32abc20a36ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186479573} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab9b2392-4e70-5610-9864-41016bfde623" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186479796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "99c3ab32-c332-5aab-98f3-193b3fead123" :question/math-academy-id "q-167500" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\frac{1}{t},t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=\\frac{1}{t},$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 2t+(-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186479889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddc5bf6-629f-5b9e-90fa-568c3a604a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186479407} {:answer/id #uuid "8496cb5b-1076-5966-83ce-efcde2ebfdc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+{e}^{2}" :db/id 17592186479554} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2317df-8f4f-5424-b64c-0f6b65e5bd2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-3" :db/id 17592186479706} {:answer/id #uuid "f6cb2b76-1e16-50f5-9407-75051eebbee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-1" :db/id 17592186479728} {:answer/id #uuid "f95e9936-472c-5c49-aaf5-2f9eb5565841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186479734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8496cb5b-1076-5966-83ce-efcde2ebfdc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+{e}^{2}" :db/id 17592186479554} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c8c22e-7185-5961-8bbd-34aaa39fe635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186479810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c6083028-59c6-57ef-95a8-453f1847efa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94889" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{y}+z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{e}^{t}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}+z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y},x{e}^{y},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=4,$ $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =t\\cdot 0+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+1\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =4+{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =4+{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}
